Chris Mikkelson wrote:
> 
> On Thu, 31 Jul 1997, Ken Haste Andersen <kenand@alf.nbi.dk> wrote:
> >g77 _is_ ported to alpha/linux, and it works quite well. It does not,
> >however, have the speed of the DEC f77 compiler for floating point
> >calculations.
> 
> Would Linux/Alpha run DU binaries?  Then, if you already have DEC f77
> (which I've heard is quite good), you could compile your program on
> a DU/DEC f77 box and run them on the Linux box.  Since they're the
> same processor, the speed advantage of DEC f77 should still hold on
> the Linux box, right?
> 
> -Chris

Caution! (1) g77 only runs under a.out Linux, I believe, although an 
ELF release is happening soon, we hope.  Anyway, I've tried building 
g77 0.5.20 on a Linux/ELF Alpha, and while it does compile with some
kludges that can be found on axp-list@redhat.com, it generates 
erroneous object code, e.g. which crashes immediately on start up
if you use a WRITE(6,fmt='..') statement in your Fortran. (2) You can
only run DU binaries under Linux/Alpha if you have a DU license for 
the box, in which case, you'd presumably run DU itself (faster and
a whole lot less buggy).  Reason, I've heard, is that by using DEC's
f77 compiler, you've linked in propietary DEC library code into your
executable.  'Course you could write all your own library functions
and get out of this. . .

