Juan Leon <leonj@coyote.psc.edu> writes:

|  The modem can be set to COM3 and COM4 as well.  I tried that.  For example,
| it is currently set to COM3 addr, IRQ 5 (I tried COM4 and IRQ 6 as well, in
| all four combinations.)  I do "setserial /dev/cua2 irq 5" and this does
| not change anything.  

Hm.  I have a serial card working fine with a Noname on COM3 and 4,
so don't give up.  Here are some things I remember:

    setserial -g /dev/ttyS?

will tell you if it is seeing the ports -- if it says something like

    /dev/ttyS0, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x03f8, IRQ: 4
    /dev/ttyS1, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x02f8, IRQ: 3
    /dev/ttyS2, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x03e8, IRQ: 4
    /dev/ttyS3, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x02e8, IRQ: 3

then the kernel is finding them and assigning them their default, 
conflicting, IRQs.  If you use

    /bin/setserial /dev/ttyS0 auto_irq autoconfig
    /bin/setserial /dev/ttyS1 auto_irq autoconfig
    /bin/setserial /dev/ttyS2 auto_irq autoconfig
    /bin/setserial /dev/ttyS3 auto_irq autoconfig

it should autoprobe the IRQ's and then setserial -g says e.g.

    /dev/ttyS0, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x03f8, IRQ: 4
    /dev/ttyS1, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x02f8, IRQ: 3
    /dev/ttyS2, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x03e8, IRQ: 9
    /dev/ttyS3, UART: 16550A, Port: 0x02e8, IRQ: 5

You should at least be able to find out which (port or irq) it's having
trouble with.



