: On 19 Jul 1997 13:20:10 GMT, KillerLoop <tplatzer@edusrv.edu.uni-klu.ac.at> wrote:
: > 
: >does anybody have any data on the speed of linux compared to DU? or NT, for
: >that matter (which we sure as hell won't install at all, just for statistics).

: This depends to a very large degree on the individual software package;  in
: general, both are similar in my experience.

: The one area in which DU really excels is compilation.  Digital's cc can be
: as much as twice as fast as gcc/alpha to compile, and the compiled code is
: better optimized as well.  This alone (well, this and AdvFS) has made me
: stick with Digital Unix, even though the freedom and better device support
: of Linux are both appealing.

The Digital compilers (fortran in particular) are certainly much better
optimized for the alpha than gcc/g77 are. However, if you have multiple
alpha machines, you only need one with Digital Unix since Linux runs
DU executables with no problem.

The only problem we've had with Linux/alpha is its NFS i/o is much slower
than NFS on DU. In fact, a program that takes a few seconds to run can
wait for several minutes for writing the output thru NFS.

Xiaoguang Zhang



