Leslie F. Donaldson wrote:
> 
> Here a question on Open Gl/mesa Hardware.
> 
> I currently have an DEC Alpha running Linux. I am looking for an
> accelerator card for the mesa/OpenGl Library.
> According to the Mesa Web site there are Intel drivers for
> 
>    Glint
>    3Dfx
> 
> I was wondering if anyone has used these  and  has anyone ported them to
> AXP/Linux.
> 

On www.quantum3d.com, Quantum3d (somehow related to 3Dfx - they sell
cards based on 3Dfx chipsets) promises support for Glint on
Linux/Intel and Linux/Alpha in 2H97.

Are you aware that the 3Dfx cards are intended for playing games, and
have maximal resolutions of 640*480 with doublebuffer, Z-Buffer and
16 bit colourdepth (some of the more expensive cards by quantum3d
support 800*600) ?

Also they run in FULLSCREEN only (there exists a hack to copy the
image from the 3dfx card to your normal framebuffer, but it's slow).

The Voodoo Rush chipset, which supports "3D in a window" is not
yet supported by Glide/Linux.

In order to provide high speed 3D in an X-Window in Linux, the following
has still to be done:
- support Voodoo Rush in Glide/Linux
- support the 2D chipset used in the Voodoo Rush based cards (AT3D,
  I think) under XFree
- coordinate hardware accesses to the card by XFree and Glide

The last one seems to be the most challenging to be done in a good
(as general as possible) way.
There are 2 basic ideas on how to get it done:
- the software uses the GLX protocol to send all the OpenGL commands
  to the X-Server, who runs Mesa (in a second thread, if possible) and
  coordinates all the hardware accesses.

  This is slow, because all calls to the graphic hardware need to be
  encoded and then be decoded again.

- do it the SGI way: the operating system virtualizes the access
  to the graphics hardware, the X-Server has to call special syscalls
  when he changes something with the 3D-window (moves it, sizes it,...)
  This may need special hardware capabilities to be truly efficient.

(There is a link to a detailed discussion of these topics somewhere on
http://reality.sgi.com/mjk_asd/opengl_links.html - I think)

Considering the Glint port, I once tried to download the code (just to
see what it does) - but the download link in the webpage did not work.
Also I never read about anyone really using this driver, so I guess
that it is not completed.

Martin
