Once again someone speaking who maybe should look a bit closer at the 
*technical* documentation available at Digital's variuos websites.
(Other than the many obscure manufaturers found in the usual PC business,
Digital indeed *does* provide a lot of info!)
Fact is: There are several CPU's out there (or rather Alpha CPU families),
starting with the old 21066, then 21064, 20164A, the fast ones then 21164 and 
21164A, and now the 21164PC with a different cache etc.

While there are differences between these chips, this has nothing primarily 
to do with OSes. However, there is something more in there: Some programmable
logic. So identical (in HW) boards will differ in PALCode, but PALCode 
(=firmware) can be loaded and is done so on a routine basis in upgrades.
Another thing then is the console. This is something not too different to
a PC's BIOS and BIOS setup procedure: Ie the intelligence on the board that 
allows you to do variuos tests, set bootflags, allows to boot via net etcpp.
There are TWO console varieties: ARC (the blue-ish, menu oriented one for NT)
and SRM (for DECUnix and VMS). Most of the boards do have BOTH consoles and 
you can switch by command (and sometimes powercycling). A few boards have 
not enough NVRAM for both, so switching consoles might mean to load the other
varietey from eg. CD or diskette.

If we are talking a systems fitness for various OSes, we are mailny 
talking console and PALCode on the system board (or rather boards: 
Sometimes the main board and the CPU board(s) are seperate, and both 
contain firmware). And then of course that every single component
in the total system is supported by the drivers is crucial.

These points really are what determines what OS you can run.

NOT the CPU does (so far) prevent running NT and Linux on the 3000 family,
but the driver support for these turbochannel systems. Same holds (so far)
for VMS on the PC-boards (eg. the PW500 systems).


I guess someone really ought to put this in the FAQ, if it is not already.

Regards

UL



