In <Pine.LNX.3.95.970811010025.6045B-100000@alpha.privatenet.arg>, on
08/11/97 
   at 01:07 AM, hfire <hfire@baraboo.com> said:

>The Alpha 166 UDB is a nice machine, however there are some things
>you should know before purchasing one:

I have one already, and so will comment:

>4. The case is VERY compact, and it's unlikely you'll be able to fit
>a decent hard drive inside the thing. 

You need a 2.5" drive as typically are used in laptops if you want an
internal drive.  I'd recommend against it however both as a source of
heat in a largely convection vented case, and as unneeded given cheap
external SCSI enclosures and drives (eg $180 for an 2Gig HP C3010MA,
about $50 more for the same drive in a case).

>This means, you must use an
>all-external SCSI configuration, which is very expensive. 

Huh?

SCSI cases are not incredibly cheap, but are also not expensive. 
Examples:  from my last local market survey (see ba.market.computers)
5.25" full height case $60 new or $40 used, 5.25" twin full height
$110 new or $45 used, 3.5" cases tend to be slighty cheaper.  Cases
with drives in them already are even cheaper still (see above).

If purpose built SCSI cases are too expensive, PC cases work just as
well if not better.  A decent PC case with a good power supply can be
had for under $40.  Add in a SCSI ribbon ($20 from your local cable
shop) and you've enough space for at least 4 sometimes 8+ devices.

>Also be
>aware that the back peripheral panel of the UDB is very compact as
>well, and depending on the type of cables you plug into it, they may
>not all fit. (big bulky D-connectors are a no-no)

True.  If you are going to use the serial ports, get the serial
splitter cable made with a low-clearance D-shell.  For the SCSI cable,
get one of the metallic shell cables, as they are smaller than the
common poly wrapped plugs.

