I got the exact same thing on my Noname with 16 MB.  In my case the problem
was that Milo was detecting 24 MB (watch all the config stuff as it scrolls
by).  I swapped email with the Milo maintainer and he said that that's the
default it uses if it can't autodetect the RAM on the machine, but a quick
scan of the Milo sources suggests to me that the reason it failed to detect
the RAM is because it never even TRIED.  Maybe I'm misreading the code here
but it looks pretty weak, especially because I can find no mention of it in
any of the installation instructions.  Anyway the fix is to type
"setenv MEMORY_SIZE 16" at the "MILO>" prompt (even though this does more
than simply create a text environment variable, strange), then when you boot
the kernel (which for some reason trusts Milo's assessment of system memory)
it won't try to load the RAMdisk into memory which doesn't exist.  Never mind
the fact that the SRM console knew all along it was just 16 MB.

If you get much farther than this I'd love to hear how -- I want to install
over ethernet from my Intel Linux box but Alpha Linux sees my PCI NE2000
clone as an "unknown PCI device" or something like that, then when the
RedHat install program prompts me for a NIC type and tries to load a driver
module for NE2000, it fails claiming /sbin/insmod doesn't exist (which seems
to be true), I don't know if that means it should be added to the RAMdisk
image (where can I find a precompiled insmod?) or whether the installation
kernel has no business messing with modules in the first place and I need to
get a NIC card that the kernel autodetects correctly on startup.

